          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            It is common in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            to speak of
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                objects enjoying “extra properties” (for instance a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                being
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            %li
              %p
                objects carrying “extra
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                carrying a
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
            %li
              %p
                objects being equipped with “extra stuff” (for instance a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                being equipped with a
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            there is a formalization of what it means to equip a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
          %p
            Now
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is precisely the kind of mathematical meta-theory that allows a nice and general formalization of such matters. Here we discuss such a formalization, due to (
            %a{:href => "#BaezBartelsDolan98"}> Baez-Bartels-Dolan 98
            , see also
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#BaezShulman06"} Baez-Shulman 06, section 2.4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            that compare a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            with extra structure/property/stuff to the underlying category of objects without.
          %p
            In fact this formalization involves the generalization of what in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+tower"} Postnikov tower
            theory (or
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n-connected%2C+n-truncated%29+factorization+system"} (n-connected, n-truncated) factorization system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+type+theory"}> directed homotopy types
            ).
          %p
            For related discussion see also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+type"} structure type
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff+type"} stuff type
          %p
            Note that this account operates under the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+n-category"} strictness
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            To begin with, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %strong forgets nothing
              if it is an
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %strong forgets only properties
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %strong forgets at most structure
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %strong forgets at most stuff
              regardless.
          %p
            Another way to break down the possibilities (used in a
            = succeed "-way" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system"} factorisation system
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %strong forgets only properties
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full
              and faithful;
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %strong forgets purely structure
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
              (on objects) and faithful;
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %strong forgets purely stuff
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is essentially surjective and full.
          %p#Strengthening
            Depending on author and the strictness of the situation, more properties are required of a functor to forget structure (
            %a{:href => "#Ehresmann57"}> Ehresmann 57
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Ehresmann65"} Ehresmann 65
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#AdamekRosickyVitale"} Adamek-Rosicky-Vitale 09, remark 13.18
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amnestic+functor"} amnestic functor
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                \-vertical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
                are
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identities"} identities
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
          %p
            However, notice that these two conditions violate the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            one might hence refer to these conditions as expressing “strict extra structure”.
          %h3#interpretation_in_terms_of_ksurjectivity Interpretation in terms of k-surjectivity
          %p
            The pattern here is best understood in terms of the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-surjective+functor"} essentially k-surjective functor
          %p Recall that for a functor between ordinary categories (1-categories)
          %ul
            %li
              essentially surjective
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
              essentially 0-surjective
            %li
              full
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
              essentially 1-surjective
            %li
              faithful
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
              essentially 2-surjective
          %p
            and that every 1-functor is essentially
            = succeed "-surjective" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \geq 3
          %p
            So the above says for a functor
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th If it is …
                %th then it …
                %th but it …
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  essentially
                  = succeed "-surjective" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k \geq 0)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} forgets nothing
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} remembers everything
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  essentially
                  = succeed "-surjective" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k \geq 1)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} forgets only properties
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} remembers at least stuff and structure
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  essentially
                  = succeed "-surjective" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k \geq 2)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} forgets at most structure
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} remembers at least stuff
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  essentially
                  = succeed "-surjective" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k \geq 3)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} may forget everything
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} may remember nothing
          %p It is worth noting that this formalism captures the intuition of how “stuff”, “structure”, and “properties” are expected to be related:
          %ul
            %li stuff may be equipped with structure;
            %li structure may have (be equipped with) properties.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-way" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th If it is …
                %th then it …
                %th but it …
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  essentially
                  = succeed "-surjective" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≠
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k \ne 0)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} forgets only properties
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} remembers at least stuff and structure
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  essentially
                  = succeed "-surjective" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≠
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k \ne 1)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} forgets purely structure
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} remembers at least stuff and properties
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  essentially
                  = succeed "-surjective" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≠
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k \ne 2)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} forgets purely stuff
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} remembers at least structure and properties
          %p
            This formalism does
            %em not
            capture the intuition so well, and in fact the ‘properties’ (and ‘structure’) remembered by a functor that forgets purely structure (or purely stuff) may not match what one expects.
          %p See also the examples below.
          %h3#generalization_to_higher_groupoids Generalization to higher groupoids
          %p
            The formulation in terms of
            = succeed "-surjectivity" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-category"} infinity-groupoids
            = succeed "-stuff," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
          %h3#generalization_to_categories_and_higher_categories Generalization to categories and higher categories
          %p
            The theory is easiest when restricted to groupoids as above; for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} underlying groupoids
            of the categories in question.
          %p
            To tell the difference, ask yourself whether the difference between a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroup"} semigroup
            is the
            %em structure
            of being equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+element"} identity element
            or only the
            %em property
            that an identity element exists. Note that an identity element, if it exists, must be unique and must be preserved by semigroup isomorphisms and by monoid homomorphisms but not by semigroup homomorphisms.
          %p
            A third option is to define a new notion: a functor
            %strong forgets at most property-like structure
            if it is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonic+functor"} pseudomonic
          %p
            Property-like structure becomes much more prevalent for higher categories. For example, the forgetful functor from the 2-category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+categories"} cartesian monoidal categories
            (and product-preserving functors) to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is essentially
            = succeed "-surjective," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k\ge 2)
            = succeed "-surjective;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k\ge 1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+2-monad"} lax-idempotent 2-monad
          %p
            Note that property-like structure is known in traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            as
            %strong categorical
            structure. Obviously, this term can be confusing in
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorial+logic"} categorial logic
          %h2#AFactorizationSystem A factorisation system
          %p
            Just as the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            has the best known
            = succeed "-way" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system"} factorisation system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            is factored into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            followed by an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ternary+factorisation+system"} ternary factorisation system
          %p
            Specifically, given a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-image"} 1-image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mi im
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 im F
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            be the category whose objects are objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and whose morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to y
            are morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x) \to F(y)
            in
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-image"} 2-image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi im
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 im F
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            be the category whose objects are objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and whose morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to y
            are morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b: F(x) \to F(y)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = F(a)
            for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a: x \to y
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi im
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 im F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            itself is equality of morphisms.) If you want to be complete, call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            itself the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-image"} 3-image
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-image"} 0-image
          %p The situation looks like this:
          %table
            %thead
