                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            can be factored into functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                  %mn 2
                  %mi im
                  %mi F
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 1
                  %mn 1
                  %mi im
                  %mi F
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 0
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \stackrel{F_2}\to 2 im F \stackrel{F_1}\to 1 im F \stackrel{F_0}\to D ,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
            forgets purely stuff,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_1
            forgets purely structure, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0
            forgets only properties. Conversely, this ternary factorization suffices to determine the notions of faithful, full, and essentially surjective functors: see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ternary+factorization+system"} ternary factorization system
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#the_classical_categories_of_sets_with_extra_structure The classical categories of sets with extra structure
          %p
            The classical examples are the forgetful functors to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            that define the classical categories such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            %em sets equipped with extra structure
            (e.g. with a topology, with a group structure, etc). Accordingly the obvious functors to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            are
          %ul
            %li faithful
            %li not full.
          %p Hence indeed, by the above yoga, they forget this extra structure but remember the stuff in question (the underlying set).
          %h3#more_examples More examples
          %p
            The embedding of abelian groups into all groups,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F :
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            is faithful and full, but not essentially surjective. Hence it should remember stuff and structure but forget properties. Indeed, the property it forgets is the property “is abelian” which is a property of the group
            %em structure
            sitting on the underlying set of a group. Hence the sequence of functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ab \to Grp \to Set \to pt
          %p
            (with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} pt
            the terminal category) successively forgets first a property (being abelian) then structure (the group structure on a set) then stuff (the underlying set).
          %p
            Notice that the order here is backwards from the automatic factorisation given by the
            = succeed "-way" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp \to Set
            is not essentially surjective.) Indeed, the above factorisation is arbitrary; it comes from seeing an abelian group as a group with an extra property and a group as a set with extra structure, but one may view things differently (for example, that an abelian group is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            with extra property, or a set with two group structures that are related as in the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"} Eckmann-Hilton argument
          %p
            The automatic factorisation is in fact like this (up to
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} pt
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} pt
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ab \to \pt \to \pt \to \pt
          %p
            because the original functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab \to \pt
            is already essentially surjective and full. In other words, from the perspective of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} pt
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pt
          %p
            More interestingly, we can factor the forgetful functor
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab \to Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ab \to Ab \to Set \setminus \{\empty\} \to Set
          %p
            Here, the first part is trivial because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab \to Set
            is faithful. The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \setminus \{\empty\}
            is the category of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited set
            %em property
            that it is inhabited and the
            %em structure
            of an abelian group but no additional
            = succeed "." do
              %em stuff
          %p
            For something interesting at every level, take the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab \times Ab \to Set
            that takes the underlying set of the first abelian group. This factors as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ab \times Ab \to Ab \to Set \setminus \{\empty\} \to Set
          %p
            So a pair of abelian groups (from the perspective of the underlying set of the first one) consists of the
            %em property
            that the set is inhabited, then the
            %em structure
            of an abelian group on that set, and finally extra
            %em stuff
            consisting of the entire second group.
          %h2#logical_interpretation Logical interpretation
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            a property
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is given by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            of the statement “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            has property
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            (a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            (a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "-stuff" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoid"} groupoid
          %p
            Now, if a property of objects of a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            that respects the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            holds for some object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi iso
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_iso
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to the groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi TV
                    %mi iso
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV_iso
            of truth values. Given such a predicate functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_P
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            consisting of those objects with the property; the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inclusion+functor"} inclusion functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi P
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_P \hookrightarrow C
            is fully faithful, as it should be for extra property. Conversely, given a fully faithful functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: D \to C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            that respects the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            as follows: an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has the property if there is some object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \cong F(a)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi P
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_P \hookrightarrow C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            back; if you start with an arbitrary fully faithful
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: D \to C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
